1
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:49,060
CONVERSATION 1
Hi, David.

2
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:52,400
I haven’t seen you in class for almost two weeks.

3
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:55,520
We thought you had disappeared on holiday early or something.

4
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,060
Hi, Sarah.

5
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:00,200
Well, it’s a bit of a long story I’m afraid.

6
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:07,940
I got a throat infection last week and had to go to the hospital to get some antibiotics as I really wasn’t getting any better.

7
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:08,980
Oh yeah.

8
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:12,080
There have been so many viruses going around this winter.

9
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:15,860
The weather has been so awful for the last few weeks.

10
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:29,380
And, on the way back from the hospital, I slipped on some ice and fell and then had to go to the hospital to get an X-ray because I basically thought I broke my wrist, although, thankfully, it’s not broken.

11
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:33,160
But I need to be careful with it for the next few weeks.

12
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,020
Oh, that’s too bad!

13
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:36,820
How unfortunate!

14
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:42,960
To make things worse, I managed to fall right in front of four girls from the ninth grade.

15
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:45,860
So I was utterly humiliated.

16
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:50,160
Plus, the laptop in my bag was broken too.

17
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:52,380
No, what a complete catastrophe!

18
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:54,900
Is the laptop still under warranty?

19
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:02,540
If it is, then you can easily send it back to the manufacturer, and they'll send you a brand-new one for free surely.

20
00:02:02,540 --> 00:02:06,340
The warranty ran out three days before I broke it.

21
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:12,920
And all my essays are in there, and I need to hand them in before we break for the Christmas holidays.

22
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:18,160
Listen, I have the number of a really good, affordable computer repair shop at home.

23
00:02:18,160 --> 00:02:23,420
My dad has used this guy before and he can work miracles.

24
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:29,760
Let’s go back to my house and we can call the repair shop, and you can have some tea and cookies too.

25
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:30,960
Wow, thanks, Sara!

26
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:32,260
That would be great!

27
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:44,540
Let me just call my mom and let her know I’ll be home a little bit later.

28
00:02:44,540 --> 00:03:02,680
Q1. What did Sara think David was doing for the last two weeks?

29
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:20,960
Q2. What happened to David on his way back from the hospital?

30
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:39,080
Q3. What does Sarah say they should do with the damaged computer?

31
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:56,280
Q4. What does Sarah say she is going to do?

32
00:03:56,280 --> 00:04:00,840
CONVERSATION 2
Welcome to this week’s episode of Book Talk.

33
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:08,440
With me today is Heidi Brown, a historian who has written five critically acclaimed books about military history.

34
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:10,620
Thanks for having me, John.

35
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:15,880
I’m so excited to talk about my latest book, which was published last month.

36
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:20,280
So this book is a novel, your first attempt at that genre.

37
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:22,500
I thought it was a bit of a departure for you.

38
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:26,980
I’d say it’s a major departure as it’s not just a work of fiction.

39
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:30,040
It’s set 200 years in the future.

40
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:30,760
Right.

41
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:32,500
So how did that happen?

42
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:38,600
You spent three decades writing about the past and focusing on the 18th and 19th centuries.

43
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:42,120
And now you're speculating about the future.

44
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:54,660
Well, after years of researching soldiers and chronicling their lives during battle, I just started wondering about other facets of their lives, especially their personal lives.

45
00:04:54,660 --> 00:04:55,900
I can see that.

46
00:04:55,900 --> 00:05:04,720
Your novel is about soldiers and it focuses on their relationships，especially the bond between sons and mothers and men and their wives.

47
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:06,100
Yes.

48
00:05:06,100 --> 00:05:11,260
That focus came about when I still intended to write another book of history.

49
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:17,600
I started by researching soldiers’ actual personal lives, studying their letters home.

50
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:20,720
So how did that history book become a novel?

51
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:25,280
Well, I realized that the historical record was incomplete.

52
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:31,780
So I’d either have to leave a lot of gaps or make a lot more assumptions than a historian should.

53
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:38,460
But why write a novel set in the future when your credentials are perfect for a historical novel?

54
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:44,400
As a historian, any historical novel you write would have a lot of credibility.

55
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:56,600
I felt too constrained working with the past, like what I wrote needed to be fact as opposed to fiction, but writing about the future gave me more freedom to imagine, to invent.

56
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:10,860
Well, having read your book, I’m glad you made that choice to move into fiction.

57
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:26,960
Q5. What does the man say about the woman?

58
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:45,980
Q6. What does the woman say about her newly published book?

59
00:06:45,980 --> 00:07:03,660
Q7. What did the woman do before writing her new book?

60
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:48,640
Q8. What does the woman say about her writing history books?

61
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:59,520
PASSAGE 1
Whether it’s in the hands of animated polar bears or Santa Claus, there’s one thing you'll find in nearly all ads for Coca-Cola: the characteristic glass bottle.

62
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:05,080
Most Americans don’t drink soda out of the glass bottles seen in Coke’s ads anymore.

63
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:14,100
But this week, the company is celebrating a century of the bottle that’s been sold in more than 200 countries.

64
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:19,740
Flash back to 1915, when a bottle of Coca-Cola cost just a nickel.

65
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:28,780
As the soft drink gained in popularity, it faced a growing number of competitors—counterfeits even trying to copy Coke’s logo.

66
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:37,960
So, according to Coca-Cola historian Ted Ryan, the company decided to come up with packaging that couldn’t be duplicated.

67
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:42,420
A product request was sent to eight different glass makers.

68
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:51,700
Workers at the Root Glass Company got the request and began flipping through the encyclopedia at the local library, landing on cocoa seed.

69
00:08:51,700 --> 00:09:00,320
Though cocoa seed is not an ingredient of the soda, they designed their bottle based on the seed’s shape and large middle.

70
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:14,660
It won over Coke executives in Atlanta and would go on to receive its own trademark, spur collections and earn Coca-Cola an iconic image that made it part of American culture for a century.

71
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:19,100
It was 100 years ago this week that the bottle earned a patent.

72
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:24,200
By World War II, Coke bottle sales had ballooned into billions.

73
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:43,895
Americans mostly consume Coke out of aluminum or plastic today, but the glass bottle remains a symbol of America that’s readily recognized around the world.

74
00:09:43,895 --> 00:10:00,560
Q9. What does the passage say appears in almost all ads for Coca-Cola?

75
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:19,840
Q10. Why did the Coca-Cola Company decide to have special packaging designed?

76
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:38,920
Q11. What do we learn about the Coca-Cola bottle designed by the Root Glass Company?

77
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:45,500
PASSAGE 2
Research shows that a few moments of conversation with a stranger creates a measurable improvement in mood.

78
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:51,840
But most of us are reluctant to start these conversations because we presume the opposite.

79
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:59,660
In an experiment, commuters who talked to nearby strangers found their commute more enjoyable than those who didn’t.

80
00:10:59,660 --> 00:11:05,580
They were asked to predict whether they’d enjoy the commute more if they conversed with other people.

81
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:10,860
Intriguingly, most expected the more solitary experience to be more pleasurable.

82
00:11:10,860 --> 00:11:12,500
Why is this?

83
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:16,520
Social anxiety appears to be the problem.

84
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:24,700
People’s reluctance to start conversations with nearby strangers comes partly from “underestimating others’ interest in connecting”.

85
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:33,000
The sad thing is that people presume that a nearby stranger doesn’t want to converse and don’t start a conversation.

86
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:41,300
Only those who forced themselves to chat because it was required by the experiment found out what a pleasant experience it could be.

87
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:43,940
Human beings are social animals.

88
00:11:43,940 --> 00:11:52,000
Those who misunderstand the impact of social interactions may not, in some contexts, be social enough for their own well-being.

89
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,660
You should be chatting with the strangers you encounter.

90
00:11:54,660 --> 00:11:59,420
You may occasionally have a negative encounter that might stick in your memory.

91
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:04,020
This is because the human brain is biased to dwell on negative events.

92
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:10,060
But starting conversations with strangers is still well worth the risk of rejection.

93
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:15,460
It may surprise you that conversing with strangers will make them happier too.

94
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:17,960
The pleasure of connection seems contagious.

95
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:33,751
People who are talked to have equally positive experiences as those who initiate a conversation.

96
00:12:33,751 --> 00:12:52,700
Q12. What does research show about a conversation between strangers?

97
00:12:52,700 --> 00:13:11,560
Q13. What prevents people from starting a conversation with strangers?

98
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:30,660
Q14. Why does a negative encounter with strangers stick in one’s memory?

99
00:13:30,660 --> 00:14:14,600
Q15. What does the passage say the pleasure of connection seems to be?

100
00:14:14,600 --> 00:14:21,820
RECORDING 1
The Caribbean islands are divided into two worlds, a rich one and a poor one.

101
00:14:21,820 --> 00:14:25,720
This tropical region’s economy is based mainly on farming.

102
00:14:25,720 --> 00:14:27,900
Farmers are of two types.

103
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:32,900
One is the plantation owner who may have hundreds of thousands of acres.

104
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:38,960
In contrast, the small cultivator is working only a few acres of land.

105
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:44,180
Most visitors to the Caribbean are rich, like the plantation owner.

106
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:52,680
They do not realize or do not want to realize that many farm families barely managed to get by on what they grow.

107
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:55,700
The Caribbean produces many things.

108
00:14:55,700 --> 00:14:58,440
Sugar is the main product.

109
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:07,700
Other export crops are tobacco, coffee, bananas, spices and citrus fruits, such as orange, lemon or grapefruit.

110
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:14,280
From the West Indies also come oil, mineral pitch and many forest products.

111
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:17,780
Jamaica’s aluminum ore supplies are the world’s largest.

112
00:15:17,780 --> 00:15:21,440
Oil comes from Trinidad, Aruba and Curacao.

113
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:25,420
But for many of the smaller islands, sugar is the only export.

114
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:33,060
Rum, a strong alcoholic drink, which is distilled from sugar cane, is also an export.

115
00:15:33,060 --> 00:15:36,480
The world’s best rum comes from this area.

116
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:44,060
Local kinds vary from the light rums of Puerto Rico to the heavier darker rums of Barbados and Jamaica.

117
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:51,360
American tourists enjoy stocking up on inexpensive high quality Caribbean rum while they’re on vacation.

118
00:15:51,360 --> 00:16:00,560
In Curacao, the well-known liquor of that name is made from the thick outer skin of a native orange.

119
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:06,740
Ever since America’s colonial days, the Caribbean islands have been favorite places to visit.

120
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:10,680
Since World War II, tourism has increased rapidly.

121
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:23,600
Because great numbers of people go there, the islanders have built elaborate resorts, developed harbors and airfields, improved the beaches and have expanded sea and air routes.

122
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:29,600
Everything is at the resort—hotel, beach, shopping and recreation.

123
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:34,140
The vacationer never has any reason to explore the island.

124
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:39,240
As in most places, those who have money live well indeed.

125
00:16:39,240 --> 00:16:43,000
Those who don’t have money live at various levels of poverty.

126
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:47,640
But here the poor greatly outnumber the wealthy.

127
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:55,320
A visitor will find rich people living in apartments or Spanish houses at the seaside or in the countryside.

128
00:16:55,320 --> 00:17:00,720
Their servants might include a cook, a maid, and a nurse for their children.

129
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:03,880
Most of the people live well below the poverty level.

130
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:08,280
In towns, they live crowded together in tiny houses.

131
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:10,840
Islanders make the best they can of what they have.

132
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:13,880
Their homes are quite shabby.

133
00:17:13,880 --> 00:17:23,667
Sadly, most tourists never see the side of the Caribbean.

134
00:17:23,667 --> 00:17:44,220
Q16. What does the speaker say about the economy of the Caribbean islands?

135
00:17:44,220 --> 00:18:02,240
Q17. What is the main product of the Caribbean islands?

136
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:21,620
Q18. What do we learn about the majority of people in the Caribbean islands?

137
00:18:21,620 --> 00:18:29,400
RECORDING 2
Talk to anyone who’s a generation not too older and they would most likely comment that children are more spoiled these days.

138
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:41,940
No one wants to have or be around demanding, selfish and spoiled children, those who get bad-tempered or silently-brute when they are not given everything they want immediately.

139
00:18:41,940 --> 00:18:52,960
Paradoxically, the parents of such children encourage this demanding behavior in the mistaken belief that by giving their children everything they can their children will be happy.

140
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:55,940
In the short term, perhaps they are right.

141
00:18:55,940 --> 00:19:06,700
But in the longer term, such children end up lonely, dependent, chronically dissatisfied and resentful of the parents who tried so hard to please them.

142
00:19:06,700 --> 00:19:14,860
Undoubtedly parents want to raise happy children who are confident, capable and likable rather than spoiled and miserable.

143
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:26,700
One factor hindering this is that parents can’t or don’t spend enough quality time with their kids, and substitute this deficit with toys, games, gadgets and the like.

144
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:32,920
Rather than getting material things, children need parents’ devoted attention.

145
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:38,700
The quantity of time spent together is less important than the content of that time.

146
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:46,640
Instead of instantly satisfying their wishes, parents should help them work out a plan to earn things they’d like to have.

147
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:52,100
This teaches them to value the effort as well as what it achieves.

148
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:54,600
Allow them to enjoy anticipation.

149
00:19:54,600 --> 00:20:04,460
Numerous psychological studies have demonstrated that children who learn to wait for things they desire are more likely to succeed in a number of ways later in life.

150
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:10,740
One famous experiment in the 1960s involved 3- to 6-year-old children.

151
00:20:10,740 --> 00:20:19,340
They were given a choice between receiving a small reward, such as a cookie, immediately, or if they waited 15 minutes, they could have two.

152
00:20:19,340 --> 00:20:31,720
Follow up studies have found that those who chose to delay satisfaction are now more academically successful, have greater selfworth, and even tend to be healthier.

153
00:20:31,720 --> 00:20:39,740
If they fail, children should be encouraged to keep trying rather than to give up if they really want the desired result.

154
00:20:39,740 --> 00:20:52,480
This teaches them how to handle and recover from disappointment, which is associated with greater success and satisfaction academically, financially and in personal relationships.

155
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:59,360
And lastly, parents should encourage their children to look at life from other points of view, as well as their own.

156
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:16,600
This teaches them to be understanding of and sympathetic towards others—qualities sure to take them a long way in life.

157
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:36,360
Q19. what will happen to children if they always get immediate satisfaction?

158
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:56,380
Q20. What may prevent parents from raising confident and capable children?

159
00:21:56,380 --> 00:22:15,720
Q21. Why should children be encouraged to keep trying when they fail?

160
00:22:15,720 --> 00:22:18,000
RECORDING 3
It is not hard to mess up an interview.

161
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:26,660
Most people feel nervous sitting across from a hiring manager, answering questions that effectively open themselves up for judgment.

162
00:22:26,660 --> 00:22:37,240
And your chances of being more carefully considered for the job can quickly go downhill just by saying the wrong thing at the wrong time.

163
00:22:37,240 --> 00:22:40,720
The most obvious thing not to do is complain.

164
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:43,400
Employers want to hire positive people.

165
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:55,680
Talking about a previous job negatively raises concerns that you might be difficult to manage, or you might be someone that blames management for your own poor performance.

166
00:22:55,680 --> 00:23:03,200
Don’t say that you’ve moved around in jobs because you haven’t found the right fit or feel that you were not challenged enough.

167
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:07,180
Statements like these will make you sound aimless and lost.

168
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:12,080
An interviewer may well think, why would this role be any different for you?

169
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:14,480
You will probably leave here in six months.

170
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:20,500
It also bags the question of what type of relationship you had with your manager.

171
00:23:20,500 --> 00:23:25,040
It doesn’t sound like you had open communication with him or her.

172
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:37,860
Managers usually love people who can self-sustain and enable growth through taking initiative, who are strong at following through their work, and who bring ideas and solutions to the table.

173
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:47,140
If you were in a management or leadership position, when discussing your current role, never take all the credit for accomplishments or achievements.

174
00:23:47,140 --> 00:23:52,340
Emphasize your team and how through their talents, your vision was realized.

175
00:23:52,340 --> 00:23:56,840
Most successful leaders know that they are only as good as their team.

176
00:23:56,840 --> 00:24:07,020
And acknowledging this in an interview will go a long way towards suggesting that you might be the right person for the position you are applying for.

177
00:24:07,020 --> 00:24:15,000
Lastly, have a good idea of what your role will be and try and convey the idea that you’re flexible.

178
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:21,160
Asking what your role will be suggests you will limit yourself purely to what is expected of you.

179
00:24:21,160 --> 00:24:24,780
In reality, your role is whatever you make of it.

180
00:24:24,780 --> 00:24:33,400
This is especially true in small companies, where the ability to adapt and take on new responsibilities is highly valued.

181
00:24:33,400 --> 00:24:36,640
And this is equally important if you’re just starting out.

182
00:24:36,640 --> 00:24:44,760
Entry level interviewees would do well to demonstrate a broad set of skills in most interviews.

183
00:24:44,760 --> 00:24:51,880
It’s important to have a wide skill set as many startups and small companies are moving really fast.

184
00:24:51,880 --> 00:25:07,160
Employers are looking for candidates that are intelligent and can quickly adapt and excel in a growing company.

185
00:25:07,160 --> 00:25:26,920
Q22. What does the speaker say can easily prevent an interviewee from getting a job?

186
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:46,360
Q23. What should the interviewee avoid doing in an interview?

187
00:25:46,360 --> 00:26:04,880
Q24. What kind of employees do companies like to recruit?

188
00:26:04,880 --> 00:26:26,945
Q25. What is especially important for those working in a small company?
